 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_vXWxnY4X8M7fw5F3",
   "name": "Equations of a Straight Line",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Equations of a Straight Line"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the gradient of a straight line?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Find two points that the line passes through with coordinates (*x*<sub>*1*</sub>*, y*<sub>*1*</sub>) and (*x*<sub>*2*</sub>*, y*<sub>*2*</sub>)\n- The gradient *m * between these two points is calculated by"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$m=\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This is sometimes known as **rise over run**\n- The gradient of a straight line measures its** slope**\n\n  - A line with gradient 1 will go up 1 unit for every unit it goes to the right\n  - A line with gradient -2 will go down two units for every unit it goes to the right"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the equations of a straight line?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $y=mx+c$\n\n  - This is sometimes called **gradient-intercept form**\n  - It clearly shows the gradient *m *and the *y*-intercept (0, *c*)\n- $y-y_{1}=m(x-x_{1})$\n\n  - This is sometimes called the **point-gradient form**\n  - It clearly shows the gradient *m *and a point on the line (*x*<sub>*1*</sub>*, y*<sub>*1*</sub>)\n- $ax+by=c$\n\n  - This is sometimes called the **general form**\n  - You can quickly get the *x*-intercept$(\\frac{c}{a},0)$,  *y*-intercept$(0,\\frac{c}{b})$  and gradient $m=-\\frac{a}{b}$\n  \n    - $c$ is **not** the *y-*intercept in this form of the line equation!\n  - You can also rearrange the equation into gradient-intercept form\n  \n    - $y=-\\frac{a}{b}x+\\frac{c}{b}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find an equation of a straight line?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- You will need the **gradient**\n\n  - If you are given **two points** then first find the gradient\n- It is easiest to start with the **point-gradient form **`space y minus y subscript 1 equals m open parentheses x minus x subscript 1 close parentheses`\n\n  - then **rearrange** into whatever form is required\n  \n    - **multiplying** both sides by any **denominators** will get rid of fractions\n- Always check your answer\n\n  - **Substitute **the coordinates of points on the line into the equation\n  - **Make sure** the equation is satisfied with those coordinates"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Make sure you state equations of straight lines **in the form required**\n\n  - Usually  $y=mx+c$  or  $ax+by=c$\n  - Check whether coefficients need to be integers\n  \n    - This is often the case for  $ax+by=c$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The line $l$ passes through the points $(-2,5)$ and $(6,-7)$.\n\nFind the equation of $l$ , giving your answer in the form $ax+by=c$ where $a,b$ and$c$ are integers to be found.\n\n> *First find the gradient of the line using 'rise over run'*\n\n`m equals fraction numerator negative 7 minus 5 over denominator 6 minus open parentheses negative 2 close parentheses end fraction equals fraction numerator negative 12 over denominator 8 end fraction equals negative 3 over 2`\n\n> *Substitute the gradient and the coordinates of **one** of the points into  *`space y minus y subscript 1 equals m open parentheses x minus x subscript 1 close parentheses`\n\n`table attributes columnalign right center left columnspacing 0px end attributes row cell y minus 5 end cell equals cell negative 3 over 2 open parentheses x minus open parentheses negative 2 close parentheses close parentheses end cell row cell y minus 5 end cell equals cell negative 3 over 2 open parentheses x plus 2 close parentheses end cell row cell y minus 5 end cell equals cell negative 3 over 2 x minus 3 end cell end table`\n\n> *Multiply both sides of the equation by 2 to get rid of the fraction*\n\n`table row cell 2 open parentheses y minus 5 close parentheses end cell equals cell 2 open parentheses negative 3 over 2 x minus 3 close parentheses end cell row cell 2 y minus 10 end cell equals cell negative 3 x minus 6 end cell end table`\n\n> *Rearrange into the required form*\n\n$3x+2y=4$"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_zTd8cmPchJhNKgvC",
   "name": "Parallel Lines",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Parallel Lines"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How are the equations of parallel lines connected?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Parallel lines **are always equidistant meaning they never intersect\n- Parallel lines have the **same gradient**\n\n  - If the gradient of line $l_{1}$<sub> </sub>is $m_{1}$ and the gradient of line $l_{2}$<sub> </sub>is $m_{2}$<sub> </sub>then\n  \n    - If $m_{1}=m_{2}$ then $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are parallel\n    - If $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are parallel, then $m_{1}=m_{2}$\n- To determine if two lines are parallel:\n\n  - **Rearrange** into the form $y=mx+c$\n  - **Compare** the gradients (i.e. the coefficients of $x$)\n  - If they are **equal** then the lines are **parallel**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/3.1.2-Parallel-Perpendicular-Gradients-Notes-Diagram-1.png",
   "file": "assets/4a1ed61112f0-3-1-2-parallel-perpendicular-gradients-notes-dia.png",
   "alt": "Parallel & Perpendicular Gradients Notes Diagram 1",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The line $l$  passes through the point $(4,-1)$  and is parallel to the line with equation $2x-5y=3$ .\n\nFind the equation of $l$ , giving your answer in the form $y=mx+c$.\n\n> *First find the gradient of the given line from its equation*\n\n`table row cell 2 x minus 5 y end cell equals 3 row cell 5 y end cell equals cell 2 x minus 3 end cell row y equals cell 2 over 5 x minus 3 over 5 end cell end table`\n\n> *So the gradient of line *$l$* is *$\\frac{2}{5}$\n\n> *Insert that gradient and the coordinates of the point into  *`y minus y subscript 1 equals m open parentheses x minus x subscript 1 close parentheses`\n\n`table row cell y minus open parentheses negative 1 close parentheses end cell equals cell 2 over 5 open parentheses x minus 4 close parentheses end cell row cell y plus 1 end cell equals cell 2 over 5 x minus 8 over 5 end cell end table`\n\n> *Rearrange into the form required*\n\n$y=\\frac{2}{5}x-\\frac{13}{5}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_GnrrqTSJcRPKGM8P",
   "name": "Perpendicular Lines",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Perpendicular Lines"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How are the equations of perpendicular lines connected?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Perpendicular lines **intersect at right angles\n- The gradients of two perpendicular lines are **negative reciprocals**\n\n  - This means the **product** of their **gradients** is equal to $-1$\n  \n    - e.g. $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-2$\n  - If the gradient of line $l_{1}$<sub> </sub>is $m_{1}$ and the gradient of line $l_{2}$<sub> </sub>is $m_{2}$<sub> </sub>then...\n  \n    - If $m_{1}\\timesm_{2}=-1$ then $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are perpendicular\n    - If $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are perpendicular, then $m_{1}\\timesm_{2}=-1$\n- To determine if two lines are perpendicular:\n\n  - **Rearrange** into the form $y=mx+c$\n  - **Compare** the gradients (i.e. the coefficients of$x$)\n  - If their **product is** $-1$ then the lines are perpendicular\n- Be careful with **horizontal** and **vertical** lines\n\n  - $x=p$ and $y=q$ are perpendicular where *p *and *q *are constants"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/3.1.2-Parallel-Perpendicular-Gradients-Notes-Diagram-3.png",
   "file": "assets/94028e9e876f-3-1-2-parallel-perpendicular-gradients-notes-dia.png",
   "alt": "Parallel & Perpendicular Gradients Notes Diagram 3",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The line$l_{1}$ is given by the equation $3x-5y=7$.\n\nThe line$l_{2}$ is given by the equation $y=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{5}{3}x$ .\n\nDetermine whether $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are perpendicular. Give a reason for your answer.\n\n> *Rearrange the *$l_{1}$* equation into *$y=mx+c$* form*\n\n`table row cell 3 x minus 5 y end cell equals 7 row cell 5 y end cell equals cell 3 x minus 7 end cell row y equals cell 3 over 5 x minus 7 over 5 end cell end table`\n\n> *So the gradient of *$l_{1}$* is *$\\frac{3}{5}$\n\n> *The gradient of *$l_{2}$* is *$-\\frac{5}{3}$*  (don't be fooled by the order of the terms in the equation!)*\n*Find the product of the two gradients*\n\n`3 over 5 cross times open parentheses negative 5 over 3 close parentheses equals negative 15 over 15 equals negative 1`\n\n> *The product of the gradients is equal to *$-1$*, so the lines are perpendicular*\n\n**Final answer:** **The product of the gradients of the two lines is equal to **$-1$**, so **$l_{1}$** and **$l_{2}$** are perpendicular**"
  }
 ]
